<DocsLayout title="Retrieving" description="The Strategy-First Retrieval Ladder: prime, brief, query, relevant, and decision search.">
  <h1>Retrieving: The Strategy-First Ladder</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A knowledge graph query costs tens of tokens; opening a source file costs 5,000 to 15,000 tokens.
    Engrams structures agent retrieval around a <strong>Strategy-First Ladder</strong>: your agent formulates
    and verifies implementation plans from self-sufficient graph contracts before opening a single file on disk.
  </p>

  <div class="callout tip reveal">
    <strong>The Compounding Rule:</strong> Open files <em>only</em> when the graph lacks contracts or symbols.
    Whenever your agent is forced to read a file, it must write what it learned back to Engrams
    (<code>decision update --contract</code>, <code>anchor add</code>) so the next agent doesn't pay the same reading tax.
  </div>

  <h2 class="reveal">The Strategy-First Ladder</h2>
  <ol class="ladder-list reveal">
    <li><strong>1. Session Briefing (<code>engrams prime</code>):</strong> Rehydrate high-level active context, recent decisions, and open threads.</li>
    <li><strong>2. Architectural Brief (<code>engrams brief &lt;node|query&gt;</code>):</strong> Retrieve full decision text, exact interface contracts, file anchors with symbols, Git drift, and 1-hop neighbors.</li>
    <li><strong>3. Search &amp; Pre-Edit Advice (<code>engrams query --full</code>, <code>engrams advise</code>):</strong> Query with convention-aware search. If a search yields 0 hits, use the returned Miss-Guidance instead of reading raw files.</li>
    <li><strong>4. Selective File Reading (Escalation only):</strong> Open only the specific ranges and symbols the graph identified.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="reveal">1. Session Start — <code>engrams prime</code></h2>
  <p>
    One call replaces half a dozen queries. <code>prime</code> fans out across active-context tracks,
    decisions, progress, and patterns, returning a single consolidated briefing within your token budget.
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams prime
engrams prime --budget 1000 --paths src/ops,src/db.rs --tags database</code></pre>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--budget</code></td><td>Approximate token budget; lower-priority sections are shed to fit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--paths</code></td><td>Filter the briefing to items anchored to these paths.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--tags</code></td><td>Filter by tags.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 class="reveal">2. The One-Call Architectural Brief — <code>engrams brief</code></h2>
  <p>
    Assembling context around a feature previously took 5 round-trips (<code>decision get</code> + <code>anchor list</code> + <code>pr list</code> + <code>link list</code> + <code>graph neighbors</code>).
    <code>engrams brief &lt;node|query&gt;</code> collapses all five into a single call:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams brief "payment provider"
engrams brief decision:62 --depth 2</code></pre>

  <p>Example response (under 200 tokens):</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "brief": &#123;
    "summary": "Stripe payment adapter",
    "contract": "fn process(amount: u64, curr: &amp;str) -> Result&lt;Receipt, Error&gt;",
    "status": "active",
    "drift": &#123;
      "stale": false,
      "base_source": "commit",
      "checked": 1
    &#125;,
    "anchors": ["src/adapters/stripe.rs"]
  &#125;,
  "neighbors": [
    &#123;
      "node": "code:42",
      "kind": "code",
      "path": "src/adapters/stripe.rs",
      "symbols": ["StripeAdapter", "process", "refund"],
      "module_doc": "Stripe API v3 payment integration with webhook retry.",
      "line_count": 148
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2 class="reveal">3. Cross-Type Search — <code>engrams query</code></h2>
  <p>
    Cross-type full-text search over decisions, patterns, and custom data with <strong>cross-convention matching</strong>:
    searching for <code>system_user_id</code> automatically matches <code>systemUserId</code>, <code>system-user-id</code>, and <code>system_user_id</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code># Standard snippet query
engrams query "authentication"

# Embed full summaries and implementation details (kills the 1+N decision get pattern)
engrams query "auth" --full

# Project specific fields (canonical aliases like summary/name work automatically)
engrams query "database" --fields id,summary</code></pre>

  <h3 class="reveal">The Miss-Guidance Protocol</h3>
  <p>
    When a search matches 0 items, <code>engrams query</code> returns a structured <code>miss_guidance</code> object
    instead of a blank array. Agents are instructed to re-target using the nearest clusters rather than dumping raw tables into context:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "results": [],
  "miss_guidance": &#123;
    "hint": "query matched nothing; nearest clusters below — retry with one of these terms, tags, or nodes",
    "top_tags": [&#123;"tag": "gcal-sync", "count": 14&#125;, &#123;"tag": "event-bus", "count": 8&#125;],
    "token_hits": [&#123;"token": "outlook", "decisions": 0, "patterns": 0&#125;],
    "recent_decisions": [&#123;"id": 42, "summary": "External calendar sync architecture"&#125;],
    "graph_hubs": [&#123;"node": "decision:42", "score": 0.85&#125;]
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2 class="reveal">4. Pre-Edit Constraints — <code>engrams advise</code> &amp; <code>relevant</code></h2>
  <p>
    Run before editing any source file:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>engrams advise &lt;paths&gt;</code> — Fast, machine-readable constraints and current violations for your target files.</li>
    <li><code>engrams relevant &lt;paths&gt;</code> — Full decision structs with retrieval scores, anchors, and Git staleness drift indicators.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>engrams advise src/db.rs src/ops
engrams relevant --staged</code></pre>

  <h2 class="reveal">5. Fast Server-Side Filtering — <code>decision list --filter</code></h2>
  <p>
    Search decisions directly from the list command without pulling the full table into prompt context:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams decision list --filter "payment" --limit 10</code></pre>
